The function $F$ is smooth on $[0,L)$, with right derivatives at zero, and $$\label{scalar:production-convexity}
 F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=2,\qquad F''(0)=\frac43,\qquad
 F''(I)\ge\frac43,\qquad F'''(I)\ge0.$$ The zero-extended surplus $S$ is nonnegative, nondecreasing, convex, and $C^1$ on $(-\infty,L)$, with $S(0)=S'(0)=0$. It is not $C^2$ at zero.

outputs.exact_statement:
For $|r|\le1$, $$\label{scalar:entropy-quadratic-bounds}
 \frac{r^2}{2}\le\psi(r)\le Lr^2,\qquad H(r)\ge L(1-r^2).$$ The quotient $\psi(r)/r^2$ is strictly increasing for $0<r<1$. Moreover, $$\label{scalar:logarithm-constants}
 \frac23<L<\frac{25}{36}<\frac7{10},\qquad
 \frac12<M:=\frac{4L^2}{3}<\frac23.$$

The function $F$ is smooth on $[0,L)$, with right derivatives at zero, and $$\label{scalar:production-convexity}
 F(0)=0,\qquad F'(0)=2,\qquad F''(0)=\frac43,\qquad
 F''(I)\ge\frac43,\qquad F'''(I)\ge0.$$ The zero-extended surplus $S$ is nonnegative, nondecreasing, convex, and $C^1$ on $(-\infty,L)$, with $S(0)=S'(0)=0$. It is not $C^2$ at zero.

For $0<h\le L$, put $$\label{scalar:scaled-curvature-definition}
 P(h)=h^2F''(L-h).$$ Then $$\label{scalar:scaled-curvature-range}
 \frac12\le P(h)\le M=\frac{4L^2}{3},\qquad P(L)=M.$$
